# a-tower-behind-a-moat — BACKLOG C9, and it is the answer to the
# sentence `a-base-behind-a-moat.keys` used to end on.
#
# That file is this file minus THREE lines — a tower marker at (2, 0)
# and its load — so the difference in the clock is the tower's and can
# be nothing else (`plans/12` § B7's rule).
#
#     a trench alone          falls at 221, all 13 alive
#     a trench and a tower    falls at 335, NINE of them dead
#
# ── ⚠⚠ What a trench is FOR, once something fills it ─────────────
#
# `@M058` read the moat as a stalemate and it was right to: *a wave
# that cannot reach you cannot die*, so the trench bought the whole run
# and earned nothing.  BACKLOG C9 fills it, and that one change turns
# the sentence inside out — **a besieger has to STAND at a fixed
# distance and dig**, which is the most shootable thing a robot can do.
#
# ⚠ So the trench's real job is not the barrier.  It is a kill zone
# with a floor under it: the wave parks itself at a range you chose,
# for as long as it takes to shovel a metre of spoil, and the only
# question left is whether your tower kills diggers faster than they
# dig.  ⚠⚠ **The magazine is what answers it here** — thirty shots is a
# tower's whole load (`plans/16` § W4), and the base falls when it runs
# out rather than when the trench does.
#
# ⚠ **The crew are still in it** and still cannot dig themselves out —
# `salvage_at` refuses spoil (`vehicle.loft` § SPOIL IS NOT SALVAGE) —
# so the cost `a-base-behind-a-moat.keys` records is unchanged.

echo a tower behind a moat: the wave parks in the kill zone and digs

do toggle_mode

do cycle_kind
click 0 0
marker 0 0 target

do cycle_kind
click 2 0
marker 2 0 tower

do cycle_kind
do cycle_kind
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
click 11 0
marker 11 0 spawn 3

count markers 3 3
do toggle_mode

palette 5
drag -3 -2 12 -2
drag -3 -1 12 -1
drag -3 0 12 0
drag -3 1 12 1
drag -3 2 12 2
count painted 80 80

# The tower is loaded before anybody moves.
top 2 0 on
shots 2 0 0 0

# The crew, sitting on the line the player is about to draw.
#
# ⚠⚠ **`send` PINS THEM THERE, and it is a control rather than a
# behaviour** (plan 29 O1).  A crew member nobody has told anything now
# walks to the nearest order inside their own senses, so left free they
# work down the line and finish stacked on (5, 2) — and this base falls
# at **216** instead of 221.  ⚠ That is a fact about the DEFAULT and it
# belongs to `a-crew-that-finds-the-work.keys`, which measures exactly
# it; a scenario whose subject is the TRENCH has to hold the crew still
# or it is measuring two things at once.
crew 5 -1
crew 5 1
send 0 5 -1
send 1 5 1

# ── The dig ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
park 5 -2
dig on
drive 5 2
tick 6
count orders 5 5
dig off

# Park clear of the line so the trail is not rubbed out, and let the
# crew work.  A trench owes a wall's 10 s a hex — `build.loft`
# § BUILD_MOAT_UNITS chose that so the player's question is what it
# DOES rather than what it costs — so 50 helper-seconds over two
# helpers is 25 s, the same 45-tick window the wall version uses.
drive 0 0
tick 50
count orders 0 0
count painted 80 80
# ⚠⚠ The whole band, hex by hex — a barrier with ONE gap in it buys
# nothing at all (`plans/12` § B7 measured a gate at +1 tick), so
# *five* is the number that matters rather than *some*.
kind 5 -2 water
kind 5 -1 water
kind 5 0 water
kind 5 1 water
kind 5 2 water

# ⚠ The crew are in it.  They stood on the line to dig it and a helper
# on finished water cannot leave — the cost, stated where it happens.
helper 0 5 -1
helper 1 5 1


# ── The waves — identical to `an-undefended-base` ───────────────
wave 5
count alive 5 5

tick 22
wave 8
count alive 0 13

# ── ⚠⚠ What the tower makes of it ──────────────────────────────
#
# **335 ticks against the same trench's 221, and NINE of the thirteen
# die in front of it** (`@M060`).  The magazine is what ends it: thirty
# shots is a tower's whole load (`plans/16` § W4), and the four that
# are left walk in over the hex the wave dug.
fall 900
ticks 331 339
count alive 4 4
shots 2 0 30 30

# ⚠ The line, hex by hex: four of the five were dug at, one was never
# reached, and the one that went over the waterline is where they came
# through.  ⚠ A trench is filled by whoever is STANDING at it, so
# killing the diggers is killing the clock — which is the loop this
# scenario exists to show.
pile 5 -1 1.0 2.0
pile 5 2 0.0 0.0

# ⚠⚠ **And the wallet still reads zero, because the base FELL** — a
# tower buys ticks, not a win.  What C9 changed is the sentence
# `a-base-behind-a-moat.keys` used to end on: a wave that cannot reach
# you *can* now die, because it has to stand at a fixed distance and
# dig.  Where those bodies can be COLLECTED from is the far bank, and
# reaching it costs the player the same hex the wave opened.
wallet 0 0
pile 7 0 0.4 0.6

snap c9_a_tower_behind_a_moat
